        "finding": "the ceiling is never the limiting factor on its own: every layout can be kept at or below 803 K by lowering the common bed inlet (628-666 K found here), and the per-pass conversion stays 14.8-23.1% at unchanged catalyst volume. Splitting the same volume over more beds RAISES the allowable inlet temperature (628 K at 2 beds -> 666 K at 5 beds) because the first bed then carries a smaller share of the total volume. The real trade-off is with the outlet ammonia concentration: cold shot pulls per-pass conversion into the opened 15-20% band but dilutes the product gas, so outlet NH3 falls from 13.21 mol% (5 beds, no quench) to 10.61 mol% (3 beds, 15% quench) - which the opened benchmark does not measure, because it quotes per-pass conversion at a plant recycle ratio we do not know.",
        "note": "cold shot is modelled as fresh feed injected between beds and the mixture then tempered to the common bed-inlet temperature, i.e. the quench is assumed sufficient and the interbed exchanger duty is what remains after it. The reported duty is the sensible-enthalpy drop of the bed effluent to the next inlet at the SAME composition: the enthalpy credit of mixing in unpreheated fresh feed is not netted against it, so layouts with a large quench are counted conservatively (duty overstated by up to the cold-feed preheat share). Converter internals (distributor, quench control, bed hydraulics) are NOT modelled",
        "status": "[ЧАСТИЧНО] layout comparison uses the calibrated activity and the assumed bed ceiling; no vendor layout data (docs/28)"
      },
      "report_both_scales": true,
      "note": "uncalibrated (published k0) and calibrated results are both reported; the calibration is one documented scalar, not a silent refit"
